---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
import { appBySlug } from '../../data/apps';

const a = appBySlug('drip')!;
const b = appBySlug('activecampaign')!;
const path = '/compare/drip-vs-activecampaign';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'compare/drip-vs-activecampaign');
const desc = description(site, 'compare/drip-vs-activecampaign');
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Does Drip offer integrated SMS?",
    "answer": "Current activation documentation offers SMS to eligible US accounts with Support All People enabled, subject to approval and paid usage. Confirm eligibility in your account."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does ActiveCampaign backfill old recovery flows?",
    "answer": "Its Shopify integration documents future abandoned-cart sync, not historical abandoned-cart backfill; missed carts from a manual sync do not trigger automations."
  },
  {
    "question": "What should the timing check include?",
    "answer": "Record checkout eligibility, integration wait, workflow delays and the purchase exit together."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does the margin example predict lift?",
    "answer": "No. It is hypothetical gross profit with fixed goods cost and excludes other variable costs."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
<ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
<section class="py-14 md:py-20"><div class="container">
<a href="/compare" class="link">← Comparisons</a>
<h1 class="hero-title mt-6">{a.name} vs {b.name} for Shopify cart recovery</h1>
<p class="mt-7 text-xl">Checkout timing and conditional SMS.</p>
<TldrBox picks={[{label:'Evaluate',tool:a.name,reason:'Confirm account eligibility and the actual event-to-SMS path.'},{label:'Evaluate',tool:b.name,reason:'Demonstrate checkout entry, total wait and sales handoff.'}]} />
<article class="prose-shopify mt-12 max-w-4xl">
<table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>Drip</th><th>ActiveCampaign</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Primary check</td><td>Confirm account eligibility and the actual event-to-SMS path.</td><td>Demonstrate checkout entry, total wait and sales handoff.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Acceptance</td><td>Prove identity, permission and waiting-send exits.</td><td>Prove entry, queue ownership and purchase exits.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Budget</td><td>Quote the actual account, channel usage and operating work.</td><td>Quote the offered product, features and migration work.</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<h2>Check Drip SMS eligibility before drawing the flow</h2>
<p><a href="https://help.drip.com/hc/en-us/articles/48924885546125-How-to-Activate-SMS">Drip’s current SMS activation guide</a> offers integrated SMS to customers in the United States with Support All People enabled. Access requires review and sending-number registration; workflow SMS actions remain unavailable until activation. This is not a promise that every new account can send immediately. Confirm paid account eligibility, approval and recipient coverage with Drip before making SMS part of the launch plan.</p>
<p>The <a href="https://help.drip.com/hc/en-us/articles/48926725054349-How-We-Calculate-SMS-Billing">SMS billing guide</a> describes separate usage tiers based on SMS subscribers and message segments. Obtain a current quote with email, SMS and support costs. For a custom event feed, Drip’s <a href="https://help.drip.com/hc/en-us/articles/36799770823309-Segment-Integration">Segment integration</a> requires an email address as the primary identifier and exposes an SMS number field; a phone-only checkout is not automatically a usable identified contact in that path. Confirm the identity requirements of your actual connector and keep email and SMS permission separate.</p>
<h2>Count ActiveCampaign’s integration wait in the journey</h2>
<p><a href="https://help.activecampaign.com/hc/en-us/articles/360001045964-Abandoned-cart-overview">ActiveCampaign’s overview</a> requires Shopify shoppers to reach the documented checkout Information/Shipping stage for abandonment data. Its trigger wait defaults to one hour. The <a href="https://help.activecampaign.com/hc/en-us/articles/115000202150-Connect-your-Shopify-store-to-ActiveCampaign">Shopify integration guide</a> offers 1, 6, 10 or 24 hours before triggering and documents future abandoned-cart sync rather than historical abandoned-cart backfill. Manual sync can recover missed data without triggering automations. An imported order history therefore does not establish a waiting recovery queue. Sources checked November 25, 2026.</p>
<h2>Compare event coverage and message rendering</h2>
<p>Proposed exercise: record anonymous add-to-cart, identified checkout, eligible abandonment and purchase timestamps in each offered account. In ActiveCampaign, include both the integration wait and any automation delay when measuring time to send. In Drip, inspect the real event and branch conditions rather than assuming the same entry point. Save customer/store and checkout identifiers, currency, variant, quantity and return URL where exposed. Preview the actual cart block or embedded product content with multiple variants and a changed cart; verify it returns to the intended checkout.</p>
<h2>Use behavior only when the payload supports it</h2>
<p>A size that goes out of stock should not receive an unavailable-item promise. A gift shopper may need sizing or delivery guidance. Financing, bundle-discount and subscription objections need verified terms or a support handoff; neither a checkout event nor an email open proves those objections. Inspect custom field and event availability before building those branches. For a repeat customer, avoid first-order offers; for international shipping, explain the actual charge rather than inventing urgency.</p>
<h2>Make the B2B handoff explicit</h2>
<p>For a high-value basket or MOQ enquiry, assign a sales owner and carry the customer, basket and enquiry identifiers into the handoff. Define who pauses promotional sends while the conversation is open and who approves resumption. Evaluate ActiveCampaign for the sales workflow your team needs, but demonstrate the task and pause behavior in the quoted plan. Evaluate Drip for the behavior-based journey your operators can maintain, with the same proof required for custom handoffs. Neither choice is established by an unsupported automation rating.</p>
<h2>Release one queue with an approved fallback</h2>
<p>Inventory current flows, embedded forms, sender authentication, custom integrations and open carts before migration. Preserve opt-outs and permission provenance. Assign every waiting cart to one system; do not depend on ActiveCampaign historical sync to recreate old abandonment triggers. Test a duplicate event, a purchase during the wait, an opt-out and a returning buyer. Recheck eligibility immediately before sending. Have an operator approve templates and stop rules, pause the old sends before enabling replacements, and retain a rollback record that avoids replaying completed carts.</p>
<h2>Compare total operating cost and incremental profit</h2>
<p>Quote audience tiers, approved SMS usage, required automation and sales features, implementation and ongoing review time. Include rebuilding forms, event mapping, cart rendering and queue reconciliation. Hypothetical $541 basket at a 49% full-price gross margin has fixed goods cost of $275.91 and full-price gross profit of $265.09. A 13% code removes $70.33 revenue: revenue becomes $470.67 and gross profit $194.76. Fees, shipping, returns, message and platform costs are excluded. This is not observed lift or an incentive recommendation. Compare incremental contribution with an adequately sized control group rather than assigning all attributed purchases to the flow.</p>
</article>
<FaqSection faqs={faqs} title={`${a.name} vs ${b.name} FAQ`} />
</div></section>
</BaseLayout>
